\paragraph{Schema complexity in dialogue and IE.}
\looseness=-1
The Schema-Guided Dialogue dataset~\citep{rastogi2020towards} and SGD-X~\citep{lee2022sgd} document degradation with schema linguistic variation; \citet{li2023evaluating} observe count-dependent degradation in ChatGPT IE. The relationship between label similarity and confusion has precedent in visual recognition \citep{dubey-2018-pairwise-confusion}, correlated label noise \citep{collier-2021-correlated-noise}, hierarchical classification \citep{deng-2010-imagenet}, and label confusion matrices \citep{guo-2021-label-confusion,muller2019when,peterson2019human}.

\paragraph{SCS novelty.}
\looseness=-1
SCS inherits the embedding-similarity criterion from LLM-PEE \citep{llm-pee-2025}. Unlike LLM-PEE, which uses embedding similarity to partition schemas for improved extraction, our SCS/BCS metrics serve an analytical role. SCS quantifies pairwise type confusability; BCS averages all pairwise SCS values within a batch to predict batch-level accuracy. The metric is a means; the empirical findings are the contribution, enabled by a controlled experimental methodology that previous work lacked---BCS-controlled batches that isolate confusability from count. The use of embedding distance for type discrimination has parallels in metric learning \citep{snell2017prototypical} and zero-shot classification \citep{yin-2019-zero-shot-bench}. The modest pairwise $r^2$ (1.3--1.7\%) compounds at the ecological batch level to explain substantial cross-batch RE variance on FewRel (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Schema complexity beyond IE.}
\looseness=-1
Schema-dialogue benchmarks \citep{rastogi2020towards,lee2022sgd} and \citet{li2023evaluating} document count-dependent LLM IE degradation. The link between label similarity and confusion has precedent in vision \citep{dubey-2018-pairwise-confusion,deng-2010-imagenet} and label noise \citep{collier-2021-correlated-noise,guo-2021-label-confusion}.

\paragraph{SCS novelty.}
\looseness=-1
SCS inherits the embedding-similarity criterion from LLM-PEE \citep{llm-pee-2025}, repurposed from schema partitioning to controlled analysis; parallels exist in metric learning \citep{snell2017prototypical} and zero-shot classification \citep{yin-2019-zero-shot-bench}. The modest pairwise $r^2$ (1.3--1.7\%) compounds at the batch level to explain substantial cross-batch RE variance (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

